+# Design analysis for the combo variant
+# a) Concrete behavior and trace:
+#    The original task reads the minority cell's 3x3 interior directly as the global 3x3 mask and
+#    stamps the same minority motif into every active cell. This combo inserts two visible rotation
+#    stages. First, the source minority motif is rotated before use: anchors in the left region use
+#    a 90-degree CCW turn, anchors in the right region use a 90-degree CW turn. That rotated motif
+#    is the one that gets stamped into every output minority cell, so the first stage is visible in
+#    the cell interiors themselves. Second, the active/inactive mask extracted from that rotated
+#    motif is rotated again before being overlaid onto the 7x7 cell lattice: top-region anchors use
+#    a 90-degree CCW turn, bottom-region anchors use a 90-degree CW turn. Example: if the raw
+#    minority motif has active mask [[1,1,1],[1,1,0],[0,0,1]] and the anchor is in the top-right
+#    region, the output minority cells use the motif rotated CW once, but the large 3x3 placement
+#    mask then rotates that blueprint back CCW once, cancelling to the raw global arrangement.
+# b) Complexity calibration:
+#    Each stage has only two states, driven by one visible spatial cue each: left/right controls the
+#    source rotation and top/bottom controls the overlay rotation. That keeps the rule learnable
+#    from a few examples while still producing four visible branch combinations. The generator
+#    restricts anchors away from the exact middle band so those binary cues stay unambiguous.
+# c) Implicit assumptions audit:
+#    - Original code assumes the raw minority motif is also the final stamped motif. That is false
+#      here, so the fill loop must write the rotated source motif, not the raw input motif.
+#    - Original code assumes the raw minority mask directly determines active cells. That also fails:
+#      candidate checking must derive a rotated source mask first, then apply the second rotation to
+#      obtain the final large-scale placement mask.
+#    - Pattern counting still works because the input still contains exactly two visible cell
+#      patterns: the majority motif and the raw minority motif. The rotated minority motif appears
+#      only in the output, so the solver must synthesize it.
+#    - The active color assumption remains safe because the generator keeps the minority-exclusive
+#      color strictly more frequent than the shared color inside the 3x3 minority motif.
+# d) Placement legality:
+#    The transformed blueprint always remains 3x3, so the original 7x7 cell geometry and the
+#    29x29 pixel canvas remain valid. The generator uses only anchors with top/left in {0,1,3,4},
+#    keeping the 3x3 block fully in bounds and outside the ambiguous center line.
+# e) Visual observability:
+#    A human can see the first stage by comparing the input minority cell motif with the output
+#    minority cells, and can see the second stage by comparing that output motif's foreground shape
+#    with the larger minority-cell arrangement. Because the mask family is filtered so all four
+#    quarter-turns are distinct, both rotations are visible rather than hiding inside symmetry.
+# f) Visual cleanliness:
+#    The scene still uses the same divider grid plus two cell motifs at a time. No extra markers,
+#    labels, or overlapping objects are introduced, so the puzzle remains as tidy as the base task.
+# g) Change inferability:
+#    No color-id arithmetic is used. The only color roles remain separator, shared, majority-only,
+#    and minority-only. All behavioral changes come from visible geometry: motif orientation and
+#    large-scale placement orientation. The generator also rejects masks whose rotations collapse,
+#    preventing the combo from degenerating into the original task or a one-stage variant.
+# New solver assumptions checked against the generator:
+#    - The 3x3 minority block anchor never uses the exact middle row or column of the 7x7 cell grid.
+#    - The raw minority motif uses two colors with a unique majority color, and all 4 rotations are
